\c 1
\s1 Empress Vashti refuses Emperor Ahasuerus
\rem /s1 King Ahasuerus's banquet; Queen Vashti Deposed; Queen Vashti Defies King Xerxes; King Ahasuerus Deposes Queen Vashti; The King's Banquet
\p
\v 1 The¦228502 following events happened during the reign¦228506 of \add Emperor\add* Ahasuerus¦228503.\x + \xo 1:1: \xt Ezr 4:6.\x* (\add Also known as King Xerxes,\add* he¦228501 ruled¦228506 the 127 provinces¦228514 \add of Persia extending\add* from¦228507 India¦228507 to¦228508 Ethiopia¦228510.)
\v 2 In¦228521 those¦228517 days¦228516, he ruled his¦228524 empire from Shushan¦228526,\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Known as ‘Susa’ in many 20th century translations, even though earlier English translations often got it correct.\f* city.
\p
\v 3 In¦228529 the third year¦228529 of¦228531 his reign¦228531, he¦228532 hosted a¦228533 celebration for all¦228534 of his officials and for every¦228534 important person who worked for him¦228545, the wealthy landowners, the officials of the provinces¦228544, as well as the officers¦228543 who served in the army¦228538 of the \add combined empire\add* of Persia¦228540 and¦228541 Media¦228541. He¦228532 was¦228545 present¦228545 in person
\v 4 \add to host\add* the six-month celebration which, displayed¦228547 the¦228550 incredible wealth¦228550 and prestige of¦228550 his¦228552 empire and¦228553 his¦228557 personal wealth and¦228561 power.
\p
\v 5 At¦228564 the end¦228564 of that time, the emperor¦228569 hosted \add another\add* celebration, but¦228564 this time for all¦228570 the¦228572 people¦228572 in the royal fort in Shushan¦228574, from¦228570 the¦228575 nobles to¦228576 the least¦228579 prestigious. He¦228568 held this celebration over an entire week in the enclosed garden¦228584 of¦228584 the¦228585 royal palace¦228585.
\v 6 There were¦228607 white and blue curtains hanging from white and purple¦228596 cords¦228593 attached to silver rings on marble pillars¦228601. The guests reclined on gold¦228605 and silver couches¦228603 set on¦228607 a¦228608 mosaic floor made of¦228608 red and¦228611 white marble¦228611, mother-of-pearl¦228612 and¦228613 other expensive stones¦228613.
\v 7 Drinks¦228615 were¦228620 served¦228615 in¦228616 golden¦228617 goblets, and¦228621 each one was¦228623 a unique design. The¦228624 king's¦228625 wine¦228621 seemed limitless as he displayed his generosity,
\v 8 and¦228646 it was served according¦228628 to his rule: there¦228631 was no compulsion to drink¦228627, but¦228627 his¦228641 servants were to¦228643 give each guest however much they wanted.
\p
\v 9 Meanwhile¦228658, Queen¦228650 Vashti¦228649 was hosting a celebration for¦228657 the women¦228653 \add in a different part\add* of¦228652 the royal¦228655 palace¦228654.
\rem /s1 Queen Vashti Deposed
\p
\v 10 On the¦228661 last day¦228661 of the celebrations, when the emperor was¦228663 pleasantly tipsy, he¦228668 ordered¦228668 seven¦228676 of his chief servants (Mehuman¦228669, Biztha¦228670, Harbona¦228671, Bigtha¦228672, Abagtha¦228673, Zethar¦228674, and¦228673 Karkas)
\v 11 to¦228685 bring¦228685 Queen¦228689 Vashti¦228688 to him, wearing her¦228699 royal¦228693 crown¦228692, in¦228692 order¦228694 to show¦228694 the¦228695 officials¦228696 and¦228696 other people¦228695 how good-looking¦228699 she was, because¦228700 she¦228704 was very beautiful¦228699.
\v 12 The¦228707 servants told her Ahasuerus's command¦228710, but¦228706 Queen¦228707 Vashti¦228708 refused¦228706 to come¦228709. When¦228706 they reported back to Ahasuerus, he¦228715 was¦228715 furious¦228715 and¦228706 \add sat there\add* raging¦228718 \add for a while\add*.
\p
\v 13-14 Eventually¦228724 he¦228722 discussed it¦228722 with his close¦228739 advisors who¦228725 knew¦228725 the law¦228736 and¦228737 could make good decisions¦228737. (These seven¦228749 officials; Karshena, Shethar¦228743, Admatha¦228744, Tarshish¦228745, Meres¦228746, Marsena¦228747 and Memukan; were¦228729 from \add various places.\add* They advised the emperor¦228756 personally, \add so were\add* the most powerful officials in¦228732 the empire.)
\v 15 ‘According¦228761 to¦228761 \add our\add* law¦228761,’ \add he asked,\add* ‘what¦228762 should be¦228764 done¦228764 with¦228765 Empress¦228773 Vashti¦228766, since she¦228772 refused to obey my command¦228775 when the¦228775 servants passed it on¦228765 to her?’
\p
\v 16 Memukan replied¦228782, \add addressing\add* the whole¦228799 group, ‘Empress Vashti¦228794 has done wrong, and not¦228788 only against the¦228791 emperor. She¦228793 has also¦228782 done¦228793 wrong¦228793 against all¦228799 the¦228801 officials¦228801 and¦228802 people¦228806 groups of¦228804 the empire.
\v 17 \add Soon\add* women all¦228822 over the empire will hear about¦228817 what the¦228819 empress¦228819 did and¦228838 they'll¦228828 say¦228828, “Emperor¦228829 Ahasuerus¦228830 commanded his¦228837 servants to bring¦228832 Empress Vashti¦228835 to him¦228837, but¦228838 she¦228840 didn't¦228838 go¦228816!” Then women¦228824 will stop respecting their¦228826 husbands¦228826.
\v 18 Even today, the¦228842 important wives in the¦228843 empire will¦228844 hear¦228851 what the empress¦228855 did and start doing the same to¦228856 their husbands; it will¦228859 result in contempt¦228860 on her part and¦228861 anger¦228861 on his.
\v 19 If¦228863 it¦228869 pleases you \add to do so\add*, \add your majesty\add*, you should personally issue a royal¦228872 decree and¦228874 have it¦228874 added to the¦228875 laws¦228875 of¦228886 Persia¦228876 and¦228878 Media¦228878 which cannot be¦228874 repealed. This decree should say that Vashti¦228885 can never again come¦228884 into¦228886 your presence¦228886. Then you should choose a different woman¦228892 for the royal position¦228889 – someone who's better¦228893 than¦228894 her¦228889.
\v 20 Have your¦228905 decree¦228897 proclaimed¦228896 throughout your empire, even though it's¦228896 so huge¦228906, and¦228896 then¦228896 all¦228902 women¦228910, whatever their status or position, will¦228911 respect¦228912 their husbands¦228913.’
\p
\v 21 Memukan's¦228927 advice seemed like a good¦228919 idea to¦228926 the¦228926 emperor and¦228919 his officials¦228923, so that's what they did¦228924.
\v 22 They wrote¦228941 letters to every¦228948 province¦228935 using its own alphabet and to each¦228933 people¦228944 group in its own language. The¦228935 letters said¦228953 that men¦228950 should be the¦228936 masters over¦228952 their wives and children, and that a husband should be able to give orders to his¦228955 wife in his¦228955 own language \add and that she should understand and obey him\add*.
\c 2
\s1 Beautiful Esther enters the king's harem
\rem /s1 Esther becomes queen; Esther Becomes Queen; Esther Made Queen
\p
\v 1 Some time later¦228958 when King¦228963 Ahasuerus¦228964 no longer felt so angry¦228962, he¦228965 \add started to miss\add* Vashti¦228968. But¦228969 when she'd disobeyed his command, he had¦228972 made a decree \add that she could never come into his presence again\add*.
\v 2 So¦228979 some of¦228980 the young men¦228980 who¦228983 served¦228983 the king¦228982 suggested to him¦228983, “\add Your majesty, you should get a new wife for yourself.\add* You could tell¦228979 your servants to look¦228984 for¦228985 young¦228986 virgins¦228987 who are¦228983 very beautiful.
\v 3 You could assign¦228991 officers in¦228994 each¦228994 province¦228996 of¦228996 your empire to bring every¦229001 virgin¦229005 who is very beautiful here to your capital city of Shushan¦229010. They could stay in the harem for virgins¦229005 under the custody of Hegai¦229019, the¦229020 castrated¦229020 royal guardian who¦229022 takes care of¦229022 the¦229023 young women¦229023 who live there and¦228991 he could arrange for them¦228998 to receive beauty treatments¦229025.
\v 4 Then¦229027 you could decide which young¦229027 woman¦229027 you like best and¦229027 then¦229027 make¦229039 her queen instead of¦229030 Vashti¦229034.”
\p The¦229031 king¦229031 liked what they suggested¦229036, so¦229040 he¦229039 did¦229039 it¦229039.
\p
\v 5 At that time, there was a Jewish man¦229043 named¦229048 Mordekai¦229049 living in¦229046 the capital city of Shushan¦229046. (He¦229045 was¦229045 from the¦229047 tribe of Benjamin: his father was Jair¦229051, his grandfather was Shimei¦229054, and¦229048 his great-grandfather was Kish¦229057
\v 6 who'd been¦229062 taken¦229062 away from¦229063 Jerusalem¦229063 and led to¦229068 Babylon when King¦229071 Nebuchadnezzer¦229076 of Babylon had¦229068 exiled¦229066 Jeconiah¦229070, the¦229071 king¦229071 of¦229071 Judah¦229073, along with¦229064 many of¦229077 his people.)\x + \xo 2:6: \xt 2Ki 24:10-16; 2Ch 36:10.\x*
\v 7 Now Mordekai was taking¦229104 care of¦229087 his cousin, who was an orphan. When¦229080 her¦229092 father¦229093 and¦229094 mother¦229094 had died¦229101, Mordekai had adopted¦229104 her as¦229081 his own daughter¦229087. Her¦229102 \add Hebrew\add* name was Hadassah¦229084, and her \add Persian\add* name was¦229086 Esther. Esther was¦229091 now a¦229093 young¦229095 woman¦229095 and she was exceptionally attractive.
\p
\v 8 So¦229109 when¦229110 the king's¦229113 command and decree became known, many¦229117 young¦229116 women were¦229110 being¦229115 brought into¦229122 the capital city of Shushan¦229120 and placed under Hegai's¦229125 charge, and this is what happened to¦229118 Esther¦229127. (Hegai¦229135 was¦229126 the overseer of the women¦229137.)
\v 9 Hegai was¦229139 very impressed with¦229141 Esther, and he¦229145 treated her favourably¦229143. He quickly¦229145 arranged for Esther to receive her beauty¦229148 treatments and her allotment of¦229141 food, as well as choosing¦229157 seven¦229155 female servants¦229156 from¦229161 the king's¦229162 palace¦229161 and assigning¦229158 them to be¦229139 her personal attendants. He also¦229142 moved¦229163 Esther and her attendants to the best rooms in¦229141 the harem for¦229160 virgins.
\v 10 (Esther¦229174 hadn't revealed her¦229177 family ties or her¦229180 Jewishness to anyone, because¦229181 Mordekai¦229182 had¦229183 instructed¦229183 her not¦229186 to.)
\p
\v 11 Every¦229190 day¦229192 Mordekai¦229194 would walk¦229195 around¦229195 in¦229209 front¦229196 of¦229196 the¦229197 courtyard¦229197 of the women's¦229200 harem to¦229201 find out \add from others\add* how Esther¦229205 was doing¦229208 and¦229190 how she was¦229195 being treated.
\p
\v 12 Each young¦229213 woman¦229213 would get¦229211 twelve months¦229228 of beauty¦229233 treatment¦229233 according¦229224 to the¦229219 \add Persian\add* law for¦229223 women¦229225—there would be¦229231 six months of applying oil¦229236 of myrrh¦229237, then¦229211 six months of perfumes¦229240 and¦229211 other lotions \add designed for\add* women. Then¦229211 their¦229233 turn¦229212 would come¦229215, one at¦229211 a¦229213 time¦229232, to be taken to King¦229219 Ahasuerus¦229220.
\v 13 The¦229250 young¦229245 woman¦229245 was¦229246 allowed to take¦229257 whatever she wanted from¦229259 the¦229259 harem¦229259 when she went to¦229261 the king's¦229264 house¦229263.
\v 14 She'd be taken across in¦229266 the evening¦229266, and¦229270 in¦229270 the morning¦229270 she'd be¦229272 led out to¦229273 the other harem which was for the concubines¦229285. (That¦229284 harem was overseen by Shaashgaz¦229281, another castrated¦229282 royal guardian.) The¦229285 women¦229276 taken there wouldn't go¦229288 again¦229289 to¦229290 the¦229292 king's¦229292 house¦229275 unless he¦229296 had especially liked them and¦229299 summoned¦229299 her¦229297 back by¦229300 name¦229300.
\s1 Esther becomes queen
\p
\v 15 Now Esther was the daughter¦229306 of Mordekai's¦229310 uncle¦229309, Abihail¦229308, from whom¦229311 Mordekai had adopted her as his own daughter. When¦229302 it¦229314 was her turn¦229303 to go¦229316 to¦229317 the¦229319 king¦229319, she¦229321 didn't¦229320 ask¦229328 for¦229323 anything¦229322 other than what Hegai¦229329, the king's¦229332 trusted official in¦229302 charge of the harem, had advised, and¦229302 everyone¦229333 who¦229333 saw¦229342 Esther liked her¦229342.
\v 16 So¦229344 Esther¦229345 was¦229344 taken¦229344 to¦229346 King¦229348 Ahasuerus¦229349 at¦229354 his royal palace¦229352 in¦229354 January¦229359, in the¦229348 seventh¦229362 year¦229360 of¦229358 his reign¦229363,
\v 17 and¦229365 the¦229366 king¦229366 loved¦229365 Esther¦229369 more than¦229370 any of the¦229372 other women¦229372. He treated her more kindly and¦229373 more favourably¦229375 than¦229378 any of the¦229380 other young women \add who had become his concubines\add*. He¦229381 put¦229381 a¦229382 royal¦229384 crown¦229382 on¦229385 her head¦229385 and¦229386 made¦229386 her¦229386 the \add new\add* queen¦229386 to replace Vashti¦229388.
\v 18 Then¦229390 the¦229391 king¦229391 hosted a large¦229393 celebration and invited all¦229394 his officials¦229396 and¦229397 servants¦229397 to a feast¦229399 to celebrate Esther¦229400 \add becoming queen\add*. He¦229403 proclaimed that this would be a time to celebrate for¦229402 people in all of the provinces¦229402 of his empire when they wouldn't have to pay taxes, and he¦229390 gave¦229404 \add many\add* generous gifts.
\s1 Mordekai and Esther earn the king's favour
\rem /s1 Mordecai Discovers a Plot; Mordecai and Haman; Mordecai Saves the King's Life; Mordecai Uncovers a Conspiracy
\p
\v 19 \add Later on,\add* King¦229416 Ahasuerus had \add his officers\add* bring more virgins¦229410 \add to Shushan\add*. During this time, Mordekai¦229412 \add had been given a position by the king so\add* he sat¦229413 \add there\add* at¦229409 the king's¦229416 gate¦229414.
\v 20 Esther¦229419 had¦229426 still not told¦229426 anyone that \add she was Jewish\add* because Mordekai¦229432 had warned her¦229421 not to tell¦229420 anyone. \add What's more,\add* she continued to follow all of¦229431 Mordekai's¦229428 instructions¦229426, just¦229435 as¦229435 she had done¦229434 when she¦229436 was¦229436 still under his guardianship¦229437.
\rem /s1 Mordekai's Loyalty to the King
\p
\v 21 One day¦229441 when Mordekai¦229443 was¦229444 \add on duty\add* at¦229445 the¦229445 king's gate¦229445, two¦229451 of¦229445 the king's guards¦229455 who protected the doorway¦229456 \add to the king's private rooms\add* became angry¦229448 with¦229445 the king and¦229457 so they¦229457 planned to assassinate King¦229447 Ahasuerus¦229461. (Their names were Bigthan¦229449 and¦229450 Teresh¦229450.)
\v 22 But¦229463 when Mordekai heard what they were¦229463 planning, he¦229466 told¦229466 Queen¦229468 Esther¦229467 about¦229472 it¦229469 and¦229469 she¦229469 told the¦229471 king¦229471, explaining that Mordekai had given her the information.
\v 23 When¦229475 the matter¦229485 was investigated¦229475 and¦229477 \add the facts\add* discovered¦229477, both¦229479 conspirators were¦229478 impaled on¦229484 \add sharpened wooden\add* poles, and in the presence¦229487 of¦229487 the¦229488 king¦229488, the incident was recorded¦229483 in¦229484 the royal record of events.
\c 3
\s1 Haman plans to annihilate the Jews
\rem /s1 Haman's Plot against the Jews; Haman Plots to Destroy the Jews; Haman Undertakes to Destroy the Jews; Haman's Plot to Destroy the Jews
\p
\v 1 Some time later¦229491, King¦229496 Ahasuerus¦229497 promoted¦229505 Haman, one of his officials (and¦229505 the¦229493 son¦229501 of¦229501 Hammedatha¦229503 \add who was a descendant\add* of¦229511 Agag). The¦229513 king¦229496 gave Haman¦229500 a very important position¦229509—more important than any of¦229509 his¦229509 other officials¦229513.
\v 2 Then¦229517 the king commanded¦229531 that¦229521 all¦229517 his other officials had¦229531 to bow down in¦229523 front of¦229519 Haman to¦229533 honour him¦229533, and¦229517 they were¦229523 doing this at¦229523 the king's¦229534 gate¦229523, except for¦229528 Mordekai¦229535 who¦229521 refused to bow¦229526 down¦229526 to¦229527 reverence Haman.
\v 3 “Why¦229552 are¦229550 you¦229550 disobeying the¦229553 king's command¦229553?” asked¦229541 the¦229542 other royal servants¦229542 at¦229546 the¦229546 king's gate¦229546.
\v 4 Mordekai told them¦229564 that it was because¦229571 he was¦229578 a Jew¦229578 \add (and Jews only worship Yahweh)\add*. He¦229563 didn't¦229562 listen¦229563 to¦229564 their \add warnings\add* so they¦229565 told¦229565 Haman¦229566 about¦229569 it to see¦229567 if¦229571 he would¦229568 tolerate¦229568 Mordekai in¦229557 keeping refusing to bow down.
\v 5 When¦229580 Haman¦229581 saw¦229580 that¦229582 Mordekai¦229585 would not¦229584 bow¦229587 down¦229587 to¦229588 him or show him reverence, he¦229589 was¦229589 furious.
\v 6 But¦229593 Haman despised¦229593 the idea of¦229594 just punishing Mordekai because¦229600 he'd been told¦229602 that Mordekai \add didn't bow to him because\add* Mordekai was a Jew¦229615. So¦229593 Haman¦229609 decided to¦229610 annihilate¦229610 all of Mordekai's¦229598 people¦229602, i.e., all¦229613 the¦229615 Jews¦229615 throughout the¦229619 kingdom¦229619 of¦229619 Ahasuerus¦229620.
\p
\v 7 In¦229624 early April of the twelfth year¦229630 of King¦229633 Ahasuerus¦229634' reign, Haman¦229640 got his servants to throw¦229635 dice \add to select the best month and the best day to kill the Jews\add*, and¦229644 the¦229651 13th of¦229651 March was chosen.
\p
\v 8 Then Haman¦229656 \add went to\add* King¦229657 Ahasuerus¦229658 and¦229664 said¦229655, “\add Your Majesty,\add* there¦229659 is¦229659 a certain group of people¦229660 who live among¦229665 the¦229666 other peoples¦229666 in¦229667 every¦229667 province¦229668 of your¦229669 empire. They¦229679 have their¦229670 own set of laws, so they¦229679 don't¦229682 obey your laws. It's¦229655 not¦229679 good for¦229681 you to¦229685 allow them¦229685 to continue to live in your empire.
\v 9 Your majesty, if¦229687 you approve of this plan, then write¦229693 a decree saying that all of the¦229703 Jews must¦229700 be¦229693 destroyed¦229694. \add When they're dead, we can take all of their possessions, and from that\add* I¦229700 will¦229700 give 300 tons of silver¦229699 to your administrators for them¦229694 to¦229706 put into¦229707 your royal treasuries¦229709.”
\p
\v 10 \add This seemed sensible to\add* the¦229713 king¦229713 so he removed¦229712 his signet¦229716 ring from¦229717 his finger and¦229712 he gave¦229719 it¦229719\f + \fr 3:10 \ft Having the king's signet ring enabled Haman to stamp the king's seal (like a signature) onto the newly drafted laws.\f* to¦229720 Haman¦229720 (the¦229721 son¦229721 of¦229721 Hammedatha¦229723, the¦229724 Agagite¦229724, the¦229725 adversary of¦229725 the¦229726 Jews).
\v 11 The¦229729 king¦229729 told¦229728 Haman¦229730, “You can keep the¦229731 money¦229731 for¦229733 yourself, and¦229728 you can do whatever you want to¦229730 do¦229735 with¦229738 those people¦229734.”
\p
\v 12 On¦229745 the thirteenth day¦229747 of that same month¦229743, Haman¦229755 called¦229780 in the royal scribes, and he dictated a letter to them. He¦229754 told¦229754 them to¦229756 send copies to¦229760 the¦229762 royal officials¦229771, the governors¦229762 of each province, and the¦229771 leaders¦229771 of each people group \add within the empire\add*. The¦229741 scribes¦229741 translated the¦229742 letter so it could be sent to¦229769 each province using its own alphabet and to each people group in its¦229779 own language¦229779. To¦229750 show that he was¦229783 sending the letter under the king's own authority, Haman sealed¦229784 each copy of the letter with¦229743 the¦229744 ring that had the king's¦229759 official¦229771 seal¦229784 on¦229743 it.
\v 13 Couriers¦229791 delivered the letters to¦229792 the¦229796 officials in every province¦229796 in the empire. The letters said to¦229798 completely destroy¦229798 all¦229794 the¦229797 Jews, including¦229800 the children and¦229788 women¦229811, on¦229790 a single¦229813 day¦229814. That¦229820 was to be the thirteenth day of¦229816 March early in the following year. The letters also¦229800 stated that those who killed¦229799 the¦229805 Jews¦229805 could take¦229825 everything that belonged to them.
\v 14 The¦229827 letter told the¦229828 officials to¦229829 display¦229835 copies¦229827 where everyone¦229831 could see them—that way all¦229831 the¦229838 people¦229838 in¦229831 every¦229836 single province¦229833 would know that the¦229842 king had commanded this¦229842, and¦229834 they could get ready¦229840 for¦229841 when the day¦229841 came.
\p
\v 15 So couriers¦229844 hurried¦229846 off with¦229847 the letters as the king had commanded¦229847. A herald in¦229851 the capital city¦229857 of Shushan also¦229849 proclaimed what the letters said. Then the king¦229853 and¦229854 Haman¦229854 relaxed and drank¦229856 together, but¦229849 there was¦229859 \add total\add* confusion¦229859 in Shushan \add about how all of this would work out\add*.
\c 4
\s1 Mordekai asks the queen for help
\rem /s1 Mordecai Asks for Esther's Help; Esther Agrees to Help the Jews; Esther plans to rescue the Jews; Mordecai Requests Esther's Help; Mordecai Persuades Esther to Help
\p
\v 1 When¦229871 Mordekai¦229862 found¦229863 out about¦229874 what had¦229869 been¦229869 done¦229869, he¦229870 tore¦229870 his¦229874 clothes¦229874 and¦229875 dressed in¦229879 sackcloth¦229876 and¦229877 \add threw\add* ashes¦229877 \add over himself (as a sign of mourning or distress)\add* and¦229878 went¦229878 out¦229878 into the¦229879 city¦229880 centre¦229879, wailing loudly¦229883 and¦229884 bitterly¦229884.
\v 2 But¦229886 no one was wearing mourning garb was allowed inside the king's¦229891 gate, so when Mordekai reached the gate, he¦229886 had to¦229887 stay just outside it.
\v 3 In every¦229902 province¦229904 of the¦229908 empire, the¦229910 letter that said to¦229915 destroy the Jews was¦229912 publicly displayed and when the Jews heard about¦229906 it¦229921, they were incredibly upset. They skipped meals and wailed¦229918 loudly, and many¦229902 of them also¦229905 wore sackcloth¦229919 and¦229920 threw ashes¦229920 on themselves and lay on the¦229922 ground.
\p
\v 4 When¦229924 \add Queen\add* Esther's¦229929 young female attendants came¦229924 with her guardians and¦229924 told¦229931 her¦229932 \add what was going on outside\add*, even the¦229934 queen¦229934 became very¦229935 scared. She¦229936 sent¦229936 Mordekai¦229941 some good clothes¦229938 to¦229938 wear instead of¦229943 the sackcloth¦229943, but¦229924 he¦229946 wouldn't accept¦229946 them¦229946.
\v 5 The¦229951 king¦229952 had¦229954 assigned some of the¦229952 royal guardians to Esther personally, so Esther called¦229948 for one¦229951 of them—a man¦229951 named¦229948 Hathak¦229950. She¦229956 told¦229956 him¦229956 to go out and speak with Mordekai¦229959 and¦229964 find out what¦229966 he¦229954 was¦229968 so distressed about¦229964.
\v 6 So¦229970 Hathak¦229971 went¦229970 out¦229970 to¦229972 \add talk to\add* Mordekai¦229974 who was out in the¦229977 plaza¦229977 in front¦229980 of¦229980 the king's¦229983 gate¦229981.
\v 7 Mordekai¦229988 told him¦229993 everything that¦229998 Haman was planning to do and¦229985 even told¦229985 him¦229987 how much money¦229997 Haman had¦229999 said¦229999 the king¦230005 would get for¦230006 his treasuries¦230004 if \add the king commanded people\add* to¦230009 kill¦230009 all¦229990 the Jews.
\v 8 Mordekai also¦230011 gave¦230022 Hathak a copy¦230013 of¦230013 the¦230014 letter that the¦230016 announcers had¦230019 read out loud in¦230020 Shushan¦230020, that said that people must kill all the Jews. He¦230022 told Hathak to show¦230024 the letter to Esther¦230027 so that she would know exactly what it¦230019 said and¦230028 also told¦230028 him to¦230029 urge her¦230029 to go¦230032 to¦230033 the¦230035 king¦230035 personally and to beg him desperately to save her people \add from destruction\add*.
\v 9 So¦230045 Hathak¦230046 returned¦230045 to¦230048 the¦230050 queen and¦230047 told¦230047 her what Mordekai¦230051 had said,
\v 10 and¦230056 Esther¦230054 told¦230056 him¦230056 to go back to Mordekai¦230059 with this message:
\v 11 “There's a law about¦230061 going to the king¦230064 that applies to everyone¦230071 in the kingdom, both men¦230073 and women¦230074. If anyone¦230089 goes¦230076 into¦230081 the inner¦230084 courtyard¦230083 of¦230065 the¦230067 palace, where the¦230068 king¦230068 can see¦230069 them, and the king has¦230105 not¦230086 summoned them, that person¦230065 will be executed¦230091. Only if the king holds¦230094 out¦230094 his¦230090 golden¦230101 scepter¦230100 to them, then they will¦230102 live¦230102. (Everyone¦230089 in the whole empire knows¦230069 this¦230113 law.) The¦230100 king¦230112 hasn't called for me in over a month\add , and if I go in without being summoned, I could be put to death\add*.”
\p
\v 12 \add So Hathak\add* went back to¦230118 Mordekai¦230118 and¦230117 told¦230117 him what the¦230120 queen had said.
\v 13 Mordekai¦230125 told¦230124 \add Hathak\add* to tell¦230124 this to Esther¦230129: “Don't¦230130 imagine¦230132 that just because you live there in¦230133 the king's¦230137 palace¦230135 that you'll be safe when they kill all¦230138 the¦230140 other Jews¦230140.
\v 14 If¦230143 you¦230146 say nothing at¦230147 all now, someone from¦230153 some other place¦230153 will rescue¦230150 the¦230148 Jews, but¦230150 you¦230155 and¦230156 your¦230158 relatives will¦230159 not survive. Who¦230160 knows¦230161, perhaps it¦230151 was¦230161 for¦230164 a time¦230164 just¦230148 like¦230165 this¦230165 that¦230148 \add God allowed\add* you¦230166 to¦230167 become queen¦230167?”
\p
\v 15 Then¦230169 Esther¦230170 sent this reply¦230171 to¦230172 Mordekai¦230174:
\v 16 “Go¦230176 and assemble¦230177 all¦230180 the Jews who¦230183 live here in¦230184 Shushan¦230184, and¦230185 tell them to fast¦230185 and¦230187 pray for my sake—tell them to not¦230187 eat¦230189 or drink¦230192 anything for three days and three nights¦230195. My¦230200 female attendants and I will also¦230185 fast in the same way. At the end of¦230180 the three days, I will go¦230204 in to talk to the¦230207 king¦230207 even though it's against the¦230211 law¦230211. I will do¦230187 that¦230208 even if it costs me¦230186 my life.”
\p
\v 17 So¦230216 Mordekai¦230217 went¦230216 and¦230218 did¦230218 everything¦230219 that¦230220 Esther¦230224 had¦230222 told¦230222 him¦230223 to¦230219 do.
\c 5
\s1 Esther goes to the king without an invitation
\rem /s1 Esther's Banquet; Esther Invites the King and Haman to a Banquet; Esther's Request to the King
\p
\v 1 Three days¦230229 later, \add Queen\add* Esther¦230232 put¦230231 on her royal robes¦230231 and¦230234 \add went and\add* stood¦230234 in¦230235 the inner¦230239 courtyard¦230235 of¦230235 the¦230236 palace, across from the¦230238 king's¦230238 house¦230241. He¦230227 was¦230244 in the royal palace, sitting¦230244 on¦230245 the¦230247 royal throne¦230247 and facing the room's entrance¦230252.
\v 2 As¦230256 soon as¦230256 the¦230257 king¦230257 noticed¦230256 Esther standing¦230262 there in¦230263 the courtyard¦230263, he was¦230255 very pleased to see¦230256 her. So¦230255 he held¦230267 out¦230267 his¦230266 golden¦230273 scepter to her and¦230276 Esther came¦230276 up \add to the throne\add* and¦230278 touched¦230278 the¦230279 top¦230279 of¦230279 the¦230280 scepter¦230280.
\v 3 “Why have you¦230288 come here, Queen¦230290 Esther¦230289?” the king¦230285 asked¦230283. “What¦230286 do you want? Tell¦230283 me, and¦230291 I'll give¦230298 you anything you ask¦230293 for—even half¦230296 of¦230293 the empire.”
\p
\v 4 “If¦230303 it¦230319 pleases¦230308 your majesty,” Esther¦230302 replied¦230301, “please come¦230309 with Haman¦230311 today to¦230313 the¦230315 banquet¦230315 that¦230316 I've¦230318 prepared¦230318 for you.”
\p
\v 5 The¦230322 king said¦230321 to his servants, “\add Go and\add* get Haman” the king told¦230321 his servants, “and bring¦230323 him quickly¦230323 so that we can do¦230327 what the queen has asked¦230321 us.” So¦230321 the king and Haman went¦230332 to¦230335 the¦230337 banquet¦230337 that Esther¦230331 had had prepared¦230340 for¦230327 them.
\v 6 While they were drinking¦230346 wine¦230347, the¦230344 king¦230344 said¦230343 to¦230345 Esther¦230345, “Now please tell¦230343 me what¦230348 you \add really\add* want. I'll give¦230351 you anything you ask¦230355 for¦230352, no matter how much it¦230360 is¦230350. I truly mean what I am saying¦230343.”
\p
\v 7 “This is what I \add really\add* want.” Esther¦230363 replied¦230364.
\v 8 “Your majesty, if you're pleased¦230379 with me and if you're happy to¦230380 give¦230380 me what I¦230370 want, please come¦230388 with Haman¦230390 to¦230391 \add another\add* banquet¦230393 that¦230394 I'll prepare¦230395 for¦230396 you tomorrow¦230397 and I'll answer your question then.”
\s1 Haman's anger with Mordekai and his execution plan
\rem /s1 Haman Plots to Kill Mordecai; Haman's Plan to Kill Mordecai; Haman Plans to Have Mordecai Hanged; Haman's Rage Against Mordecai
\p
\v 9 Haman was¦230423 feeling very happy as¦230409 he left¦230402 \add the banquet\add* that day¦230404. But¦230402 then¦230402 he¦230402 saw¦230409 Mordekai sitting at¦230414 the king's¦230415 gate¦230414 and¦230402 noticed¦230409 that Mordekai didn't¦230416 stand¦230418 up¦230418 or show fearful reverence to him¦230422. This made Haman furious with¦230414 Mordekai
\v 10 but¦230430 he¦230430 restrained¦230430 himself¦230430 and¦230432 went¦230432 home¦230435. Then¦230430 he sent¦230436 for his friends¦230440 and brought¦230437 in his wife¦230444, Zeresh¦230443,
\v 11 boasting to¦230447 them¦230447 about how rich he was and about how many¦230456 sons¦230454 he had, and how the¦230460 king¦230460 had¦230463 promoted¦230463 him \add many times\add* and given him a position above¦230464 all¦230456 of¦230467 the other officials¦230466 and¦230467 administrators.
\v 12 “And¦230470 that is not¦230473 all,” Haman¦230471 added. “I¦230494 was the only one that Queen¦230477 Esther¦230476 invited to go with¦230478 the¦230480 king¦230480 to¦230481 a banquet¦230483 that¦230484 she¦230486 prepared¦230486 for¦230487 us today. And she's¦230475 also¦230491 invited only me¦230490 to go with the king again tomorrow¦230493.
\v 13 However, I still can't¦230505 be happy as long as I keep seeing¦230513 that¦230504 Jew¦230517, Mordekai¦230516, sitting¦230518 there at¦230519 the¦230517 king's¦230520 gate¦230519.”
\p
\v 14 Then Zeresh¦230524 his¦230525 wife¦230525 and¦230526 all¦230526 his friends¦230528 said¦230522 to¦230523 him¦230543, “Get them to install 25-metre high¦230532 pole, and¦230535 in¦230535 the morning¦230535 get permission from the¦230538 king¦230538, and¦230539 get them to impale Mordekai¦230542 on¦230535 it¦230555. Then you can go¦230544 with¦230535 the¦230548 king¦230548 to¦230549 the¦230553 banquet¦230553 and¦230555 enjoy yourself.”
\p That idea sounded good¦230555 to¦230538 Haman¦230558, so he¦230559 got the¦230560 pole erected.
\c 6
\s1 The king honours Mordekai
\rem /s1 Haman executed; The King Honours Mordecai; Mordecai Honoured
\p
\v 1 That¦230570 night¦230563 the king¦230567 was unable to sleep¦230566 so he¦230568 ordered¦230568 the royal records¦230573 to be fetched and¦230576 to be read¦230577 to¦230578 him.
\v 2 The¦230591 records¦230582 mentioned that Bigthan¦230588 and¦230581 Teresh¦230589, two¦230590 of¦230591 the royal guardians¦230593 who¦230595 protected the doorway¦230594 to the king's¦230592 private quarters, had planned to assassinate the king, but¦230581 that Mordekai¦230585 had reported¦230584 it¦230581.
\v 3 “What¦230604 honour¦230607 or recognition did¦230606 I give Mordekai¦230609 for¦230609 saving my life?” the¦230614 king¦230615 asked.
\p “Nothing was¦230619 done¦230619 for him,” replied¦230602 the¦230603 young¦230614 men¦230614 who¦230616 were attending¦230616 him.
\p
\v 4 At that moment, Haman¦230627 had entered¦230628 the outer¦230633 courtyard¦230629 of¦230629 the king's¦230624 house¦230630. He¦230623 had come¦230628 to tell¦230623 the king that he wanted to execute Mordekai¦230639 on¦230626 the pole that¦230643 he'd had¦230645 set¦230645 up for¦230635 him¦230646.
\p “Who's¦230625 out in¦230626 the¦230629 courtyard¦230629?” the king¦230632 asked¦230623.
\p
\v 5 “Haman's¦230653 out there¦230652 standing¦230654 in¦230655 the courtyard¦230655,” replied¦230648 the¦230650 young men.
\p “Bring¦230652 him¦230658 in then¦230648,” the¦230649 king¦230657 instructed.
\p
\v 6 When¦230660 Haman¦230661 came¦230660 in¦230668, the¦230664 king¦230664 asked him¦230663, “What¦230665 should I do for¦230667 a man¦230668 that I would really like to¦230676 honour¦230672?”
\p Haman thought to himself, “Surely it¦230662 must¦230677 be me¦230682 that the king would like to honour more¦230682 than¦230682 anyone else?”
\v 7 so he¦230684 replied¦230684 to the¦230688 king¦230688, “If¦230685 you really want to honour¦230693 someone,
\v 8 tell your servants to bring¦230695 one of¦230696 your own royal¦230697 robes that¦230698 you have¦230699 already worn¦230699 yourself and also¦230703 bring a horse¦230703 that¦230704 you have¦230705 already ridden¦230705 yourself and¦230708 put¦230709 a royal¦230711 crown on¦230712 its¦230712 head¦230712.
\v 9 Then¦230714 have one¦230722 of¦230722 your most noble¦230724 officials¦230722 present the man¦230728 with¦230732 the robe and the horse. Have your servants put¦230714 the robe on¦230717 the¦230719 man¦230721 whom¦230729 you really want to honour and have them seat that man on the horse and then¦230714 lead the horse through the public square¦230737 of¦230737 the¦230738 city¦230738 while they¦230739 shout out \add to everyone\add* in¦230732 front¦230740 of them, ‘The¦230745 king¦230745 is¦230742 doing¦230742 this because¦230743 he¦230746 really wants to honour¦230747 this man!’ ”
\p
\v 10 The¦230750 king¦230750 \add agreed so\add* he¦230749 replied¦230749 to¦230751 Haman¦230751, “Go quickly¦230752 and take¦230753 the¦230756 robe and¦230757 the¦230759 horse¦230759 and do what you've just¦230760 described for¦230765 Mordekai¦230765 the¦230766 Jew¦230766 who¦230767 sits¦230767 by the¦230768 gate¦230768 to the¦230769 palace. Make¦230772 sure that¦230775 you¦230776 do absolutely everything that you've said¦230776.”
\p
\v 11 So¦230778 Haman¦230779 got¦230778 the¦230782 robe and¦230783 the¦230785 horse and¦230786 put the robe on Mordekai¦230789, seated him on the horse, and then¦230778 led¦230778 the horse through the¦230791 public square¦230791 of¦230791 the¦230792 city¦230792. As he did¦230790, he shouted¦230793 out to everyone in¦230791 front¦230794 of him¦230794, “The¦230799 king¦230799 is¦230796 doing¦230796 this because¦230797 he¦230800 really wants to honour¦230801 this man¦230797!”
\p
\v 12 Afterwards, Mordekai¦230804 returned¦230803 to¦230805 the¦230807 king's¦230808 gate¦230807 but¦230803 Haman¦230809 hurried¦230810 to¦230811 his house¦230813, covering¦230815 his head¦230816 like a mourner¦230814.
\v 13 \add At home\add* he¦230818 explained to¦230820 Zeresh¦230820 his¦230821 wife¦230821 and¦230822 to¦230822 all¦230822 his friends¦230824 everything that¦230828 had¦230829 happened¦230829 to him. Then¦230818 his advisors¦230833 and his wife said¦230830 to¦230832 him¦230832, “Mordekai¦230839 has¦230841 begun¦230841 to defeat you and since he is¦230837 one¦230837 of¦230824 the¦230837 Jewish¦230838 people¦230837, you¦230841 won't¦230844 win against him¦230847—instead, he'll certainly¦230850 defeat you¦230851.”
\s1 Haman's execution
\rem /s1 Haman Is Put to Death; Haman's Downfall and Mordecai's Advancement
\p
\v 14 While they¦230854 were¦230855 still¦230854 talking¦230855 together, \add some of\add* the royal guardians arrived¦230859 to¦230861 bring¦230861 Haman¦230864 quickly¦230860 to¦230865 the¦230867 banquet¦230867 that¦230868 Esther¦230871 had had prepared¦230870.
\c 7
\rem /s1 The King Executes Haman; Haman Hanged
\p
\v 1 So¦230873 the¦230874 king¦230874 and¦230875 Haman¦230875 went¦230873 to¦230876 Queen¦230880 Esther's¦230879 \add banquet\add*.
\v 2 While they were drinking¦230888 wine¦230889 at¦230888 that second¦230887 banquet¦230888, the¦230883 king¦230883 asked¦230892 Esther again, “Now please tell¦230882 me what¦230890 you really want, Queen¦230894 Esther. Tell¦230882 me, and¦230882 I'll do¦230904 it for you—I'll give¦230895 you anything you ask¦230899 for, no matter what it is¦230892.”
\p
\v 3 “Your¦230914 majesty,” Queen¦230908 Esther¦230907 replied¦230909, “if¦230916 you are pleased¦230921 with¦230914 me, I hope you'll be¦230922 willing to¦230924 do what I ask¦230928. Please allow¦230922 me¦230924 to live¦230925, and¦230906 please save my¦230925 people¦230927. That is¦230918 what I am asking¦230909 for¦230926
\v 4 because¦230930 someone has¦230931 turned me and¦230933 my¦230933 people¦230933 over \add to our enemies\add* and they're going to¦230934 completely destroy¦230934 us. If¦230937 someone had¦230940 \add just\add* sold¦230931 the men¦230933 and even the women to be slaves¦230939, I¦230932 wouldn't have said anything, because that wouldn't have been¦230931 important enough to bother your majesty with¦230946.”
\p
\v 5 “Who¦230956 has¦230965 done¦230967 this¦230958?” King¦230951 Ahasuerus¦230952 asked her. “Where¦230959 is¦230962 the¦230951 man who¦230956 has dared to¦230954 do¦230967 such a thing?”
\p
\v 6 “The¦230977 man who is our bitter enemy¦230975 is this¦230978 evil¦230977 man Haman¦230976!” answered¦230970 Esther¦230972.
\p \add By this time,\add* Haman¦230979 was¦230980 terrified¦230980 standing there in front¦230981 of the¦230982 king¦230982 and¦230970 the queen¦230983.
\v 7 Now the¦230985 king¦230985 became so angry¦230987 that he got up¦230986 and abandoned his wine-drinking at¦230987 the¦230988 banquet¦230988 and went outside into¦230990 the palace¦230993 garden¦230992. But¦230985 Haman¦230994 stayed¦230995 inside to¦230996 beg his life¦230999 from¦231000 Queen¦231001 Esther¦231000 because¦231002 he¦231003 recognized that¦231004 the king wanted to¦231007 \add execute\add* him¦231007.
\v 8 \add As he was pleading for his life,\add* Haman¦231022 \add knelt down\add* very close to Esther¦231028 as she was¦231023 \add reclining\add* on¦231038 a couch¦231026. When¦231012 the¦231012 king¦231012 returned¦231013 from¦231014 the palace¦231015 garden¦231014 to¦231016 the room where they had been¦231023 drinking¦231020 wine¦231021, he \add saw this and\add* exclaimed, “\add Now\add* he's¦231030 even trying to¦231033 rape¦231033 the queen¦231036 in my presence and in my own house¦231018!”
\p As soon as the¦231036 king said¦231030 this, some of¦231043 his servants covered¦231045 Haman's¦231044 face¦231043 \add (as a sign that he would be executed)\add*.
\v 9 Then Harbona¦231049, who was¦231072 one¦231050 of¦231051 the guardians who served the king¦231079 personally, said¦231078, “Your majesty, Haman¦231063 has¦231062 also¦231056 set up a pole twenty-five metres high¦231075 at his house¦231073 because he¦231078 wants to¦231054 hang¦231080 Mordekai on¦231081 it¦231078. But¦231048 Mordekai saved your life.”
\p “Execute Haman on it¦231048!” the¦231053 king commanded.
\p
\v 10 So¦231083 they¦231083 impaled Haman¦231086 on¦231087 the¦231089 pole that¦231090 he¦231092 had¦231092 set¦231092 up for¦231093 Mordekai¦231093, and¦231083 then¦231083 the king's¦231095 anger¦231094 dissipated.
\c 8
\s1 The Jews are enabled to defend themselves
\rem /s1 Esther Saves the Jews; The King's Edict in Behalf of the Jews; A Decree to Help the Jews; The Jews Are Told to Fight Back
\p
\v 1 That¦231100 same day¦231099, King¦231102 Ahasuerus¦231103 gave¦231101 Queen¦231105 Esther all the property that had¦231119 belonged to Haman¦231109, the enemy of¦231108 the Jews¦231111. Esther told¦231119 the king that Mordekai was¦231122 \add her cousin and that he had been like a father\add* to¦231124 her¦231124, and¦231113 \add so the king summoned\add* Mordekai to come¦231114 to¦231115 him.
\v 2 Then¦231126 the¦231127 king¦231127 removed¦231126 his¦231130 signet¦231130 ring which¦231131 he¦231132 had retrieved from¦231133 Haman, and¦231126 gave¦231134 it¦231134 to Mordekai. The¦231143 queen also¦231134 put Mordekai in charge¦231136 of¦231143 all the property that¦231131 had belonged to Haman¦231144.
\rem /s1 The triumphant Jews destroy their enemies
\p
\v 3 Then Esther¦231148 came to speak¦231149 to¦231150 the king¦231151 again¦231147, and¦231149 she¦231149 knelt down and¦231152 put her face¦231153 right on¦231169 top of¦231150 his¦231154 feet¦231154. She¦231155 cried¦231155 as she¦231156 begged him¦231158 to¦231159 stop Haman's¦231163 terrible plan¦231166 to destroy the¦231171 Jews.
\v 4 The¦231180 king held¦231173 out¦231173 his golden¦231178 scepter¦231177 toward Esther¦231175, so she¦231181 stood¦231181 up and¦231173 waited facing¦231182 the¦231174 king.
\v 5 “Your majesty,” she¦231185 said¦231185, “if¦231186 you think that it's¦231197 the right¦231197 thing to do, and¦231192 if¦231192 you're pleased with¦231203 me, please write¦231204 a new letter¦231208 revoking¦231205 the¦231208 letters¦231208 that Haman¦231210 (the¦231211 son¦231211 of¦231199 Hammedatha¦231213, the¦231214 Agagite¦231214) sent out \add instructing the people\add* to¦231217 destroy¦231217 all¦231222 the Jews¦231220 throughout your empire.
\v 6 I couldn't bear to see the¦231241 terrible thing that¦231232 is about¦231237 to happen to my people¦231237. I \add certainly\add* couldn't bear to see \add all\add* my relatives¦231242 destroyed¦231241!”
\p
\v 7 Then king¦231246 Ahasuerus¦231247 said¦231245 to¦231248 Queen¦231249 Esther and¦231250 to¦231250 Mordekai¦231250 the¦231251 Jew¦231251, “Listen, I've¦231256 given¦231256 the house¦231253 of¦231253 Haman¦231255 to¦231257 Esther, and¦231258 they've¦231259 executed him¦231258 on¦231268 the pole because he¦231266 attempted to destroy the Jews¦231251.
\v 8 But¦231271 as you \add both\add* know, no one can revoke¦231296 a letter that¦231286 has my name and my official seal on it. So¦231271 this is¦231288 what you should do: write¦231272 \add a new letter\add* to¦231296 help the Jews¦231275, as you think best and put my name on it and seal the¦231276 letter with¦231277 my signet¦231293 ring.”
\p
\v 9 It was now June, and¦231298 the king¦231301 sent for his scribes and they¦231298 wrote¦231314 a letter to the Jews containing everything that¦231330 Mordekai¦231320 dictated. It was also¦231312 addressed to royal officials¦231328, and to the governors¦231327 and¦231328 leaders¦231328 in each province. The empire had 127 provinces¦231340, extending all¦231315 the way from¦231332 India¦231332 \add in the east\add* to¦231333 Ethiopia¦231335 \add in the west\add*. The scribes wrote to every province using its¦231342 own alphabet and¦231343 to each people¦231343 group in its¦231345 own language¦231345, and¦231346 to¦231346 the¦231348 Jews¦231348 in their¦231349 language and¦231350 using their¦231350 alphabet.
\v 10 The¦231354 letters were written¦231352 in¦231357 King¦231354 Ahasuerus¦231355' name¦231353, and¦231352 \add each one\add* was sealed¦231356 with¦231357 the king's¦231358 signet¦231357 ring. Then¦231352 the letters¦231360 were distributed by¦231361 mounted¦231363 couriers¦231362 riding¦231364 on¦231357 swift horses¦231365 bred from the¦231366 royal¦231366 mares¦231368.
\p
\v 11 The¦231372 letters stated that¦231396 the king¦231372 permitted¦231371 the Jews¦231373 in¦231376 each¦231376 and every¦231391 city¦231380 to get¦231401 together and defend¦231382 themselves, and what's more to destroy¦231386, kill¦231386, and annihilate¦231388 all¦231376 the forces¦231393 of¦231391 any people¦231394 or province¦231395 hostile to them¦231397, including¦231380 women¦231399 and children, and \add then\add* to¦231401 plunder¦231401 their¦231385 possessions.
\v 12 \add This was to take place\add* on a certain day in¦231410 every province¦231407 throughout the empire: the 13th of¦231407 March\x + \xo 8:12: \xt 3:7.\x* \add (the same day on which Haman's law was to take effect)\add*.
\v 13 A copy¦231421 of¦231421 the¦231422 decree was¦231423 to¦231423 be¦231423 included as law¦231424 in¦231425 every¦231430 province¦231427 and¦231433 to be displayed¦231429 to¦231430 all¦231430 the¦231432 people¦231432 so that the Jews could be prepared¦231436 to¦231440 fight back against their¦231441 enemies¦231441 on that day¦231438.
\v 14 So the¦231443 couriers¦231443 on¦231450 the royal¦231446 horses¦231445 rode¦231444 out¦231447 quickly¦231448 under the king's¦231451 orders to deliver the letters. The¦231452 new law¦231452 was¦231453 also¦231449 proclaimed throughout the capital city of¦231444 Shushan¦231454.
\p
\v 15 The¦231462 king¦231462 \add gave\add* Mordekai royal¦231464 clothing¦231463 that was blue and¦231466 white¦231466, with¦231463 a large¦231469 gold¦231468 crown¦231467, and a purple¦231472 robe¦231470 made from¦231461 fine¦231471 linen¦231471. The people of¦231463 Shushan¦231474 cheered happily when Mordekai exited¦231459 \add the palace\add*.
\v 16 For the Jews¦231478, it¦231479 was \add a time\add* of light¦231480 and happiness, and they were honoured¦231483 \add by those around them\add*.
